Accuracy of the system using all eight features:                    98.34%
Accuracy of the system using only the five core features:           97.74%
Accuracy of the system using only the three additonal features:     75.44%

Discussion on accuracy of the system using different feature configurations:

As can be seen by the results, the system preforms very well using only the five
core features; the three additional features only improve the system by 0.6%.
This suggests that the five core features already capture most of the readily
available information for this task. The three additional features do provide
additional information, but only marginally.

There may be a few reasons for this marginal increase in accuracy beyond the 
five core features capturing most of the information available. Starting
with the first additional feature; capturing digits to the right of a period
is useful for cases such as "Jan. 12", but may be relatively uncommon
otherwise. Second, L being capitalized is potentially useful for
distinguishing abbreviations/proper nouns, but may already overlap
with information already contained in L. Lastly, the frequency of 
L is potentially useful for Identifying abbreviations, but it may
be noisy.

Taking these assumptions, it can be concluded that outside the five
core features, there aren't many obvious features that can be chosen
that can dramatically increase the accuracy of the system. However, the three
additional features were able to achieve an accuracy of 75.44% independently,
meaning that they did capture meaningful information. In the end, more
experimentation will likely be needed to give a difinitive answer.

Decision Tree:

According to the decision tree that was generated, the feature that appeared at the
root of the tree, was the additional feature that counted the number of times L
appears to the left of a period. This is a strong indicator that this feature
provides a substantial amount of information.

I think this feature is particulary useful because it makes intuitive sense that 
if a word occurs frequently before a period, it is most likely an abbreviation/proper noun
and not a sentence boundary.
